course: everyday
lesson: 483
title: Neighbours who don't speak and the friendly shopkeeper
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Two old men on Sam's street, Mr Shaw and Mr Kowalski, haven't spoken in eleven years. They put their bins out at the same minute and look past each other like the other is a lamppost. Sam asks why. Dad says it was a fence, or a dog, or a parked car: «They probably don't know either, son.» At Mrs Kowalski's funeral, Mr Shaw turns up in his best suit and shakes Mr Kowalski's hand. Neither speaks for a long moment. The next week they're on the bench outside the shop, sharing a flask of tea. Neither ever explains.
explain: Mr Patel at the corner shop knows everyone's name, everyone's dog and who likes which crisps. On Sam's birthday there's a free lolly: «Ten already? Where does the time go.» One night his window is smashed and the till stolen. By nine next morning half the street is there with brooms, a ladder and tea; Big Steve boards the window, Mr Grimshaw sweeps glass, Sam hands out biscuits. Mr Patel cries with thanks. When he catches Kieran slipping a chocolate bar into his pocket, he doesn't call the police: «You'll sweep my shop every Saturday for a month, and then we're square» (even). On the last Saturday he gives Kieran the chocolate bar for free.
explain: Grown-ups can be as silly as Year 3s. Lily's road has a bin war: «NOT YOUR BIN.» «IT WAS ONE BAG.» «THIEF.» It lasts all year, until every house paints its number on its bin in huge white letters. Two dads shout over a parking space outside school, «You weren't, you clown!», and one of them is Kieran's dad. A teacher moves them along, and Kieran is mortified all day. You can love someone and still see they got it wrong.
explain: And grumpy and kind can live in one person. Mrs Platt, who moans about bikes and wind chimes, is first at the door when Sam's dad breaks his leg on the ice, with a casserole and a shovel for the drive. Mrs Lopez brings Mia's family tamales (steamed corn parcels), wrapped in a tea towel, every Christmas Eve: «Eat them hot, mija» (Spanish for «my girl»). Pat the lollipop lady says «Lovely weather for ducks, chuck!» on rainy days, for twenty years; when she retires, the whole school makes her a giant card. The number 9 driver says «Mornin', trouble» to Jake, and one bad day, when Jake is crying, waits an extra minute: «You alright, son?» Jake says he's fine. He isn't, but someone asked.
fact: Grown-ups can hold grudges for years, sometimes after forgetting what started them.
fact: Silence keeps a feud alive; it is never too late to make up, and sad times sometimes end old feuds.
fact: Friendly faces in your street, like a shopkeeper or a lollipop lady, make a neighbourhood feel safe and kind.
fact: Neighbours helping each other when something goes wrong is what «community» means.
fact: Taking something from a shop without paying is stealing, even if it is only a chocolate bar.
fact: Some grown-ups give second chances; take them and don't waste them.
fact: Grown-up feuds can be as silly as playground ones, and a simple practical fix can end them.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes behave childishly; you can love them and still see they got it wrong.
fact: The same person can be grumpy about small things and very kind when it matters.
fact: Sharing food is an old way of building friendships between families.
fact: Little friendly words, like «You alright?», can help someone having a bad day.
fact: A lollipop lady or man helps children cross the road outside school; «chuck» is a Northern word for «dear».
exercise: How long had Mr Shaw and Mr Kowalski not spoken? | Eleven years | Eleven days only | Eleven months | Since last week
exercise: Why did they stop speaking, according to Sam's dad? | Nobody knows, maybe a fence, a dog or a car | A big fight over money that one of them owed | A crime one of them did | One stole the other's dog and never gave it back
exercise: What did Mr Shaw do at Mrs Kowalski's funeral? | Shook Mr Kowalski's hand | Stayed at home | Talked loudly all through it | Argued about the fence
exercise: What did Mr Patel give Sam on his birthday? | A free lolly | A bag of crisps | A football | Ten pounds
exercise: What did the street do after Mr Patel's shop was broken into? | Turned up with brooms, a ladder and tea | Went to a different shop across town instead | Blamed Mr Patel | Did nothing at all and kept walking past
exercise: Kieran tried to steal a chocolate bar. What did Mr Patel decide? | He'd sweep the shop every Saturday for a month | He'd call the police and his school at once | He'd be banned from the shop for life | He could keep it for free if he said sorry nicely
exercise: What did Mr Patel give Kieran on the last Saturday? | The chocolate bar, for free | A bill for all the sweeping | A long telling-off | A letter to take to his school
exercise: How did the bin war end? | Every house painted its number on its bin | The council came and took all the bins away | Number 6 moved house | A judge decided who owned which bin
exercise: Two dads shouted over a parking space. How did Kieran feel? | Embarrassed for his dad | Very proud of his dad for it | He didn't even notice it | He joined in the shouting
exercise: Mrs Platt complains about everything. What did she do when Sam's dad broke his leg? | Brought a casserole and shovelled the drive | Complained about the noise of the ambulance | Closed her curtains | Took a photo of him on the ice and laughed
exercise: What does Mrs Lopez bring every Christmas Eve? | Tamales wrapped in a tea towel | A Christmas tree for the front room | A big box of crackers | A card and nothing else
exercise: What does Pat the lollipop lady say on rainy days? | «Lovely weather for ducks, chuck!» | «Stay inside, kids!» | «I hate the rain, it's horrible!» | «No school today, go back home!»
exercise: What did the bus driver do when Jake was crying? | Waited an extra minute and asked if he was alright | Drove off quickly | Told him to stop crying and sit down at the back | Called the police
exercise: What did the whole school do when Pat retired? | Made her a giant card | Forgot about her at once | Gave her a brand-new car | Had a whole day off school
exercise: What does «we're square» mean? | We're even, nothing more is owed | We're best friends now and forever | We're in trouble | We're going home
test: «they haven't spoken in 11 years so they never will». What actually happened? | They made peace after the funeral | They never spoke to each other again | One of them sold up and moved to Spain | They had a big fight at the funeral
test: «it's only a Mars bar, shops don't even notice». Is it OK? | No, it's still stealing | Yes, if it's under a pound | Yes, big shops don't mind | No, only stealing crisps counts
test: «why'd Mr Patel let him off, he should've called the feds». (feds is American slang for police.) Why the sweeping instead? | He gave Kieran a second chance to make it right | He didn't care about stealing | He was scared of Kieran and his mates | He wanted free cleaning every Saturday forever
test: «NOT YOUR BIN. THIEF.» Is this grown-up feud more serious than a playground one? | No, it's just as silly, and a simple fix ended it | Yes, bins are very important | Yes, it should go to court | No, so they should keep fighting
test: «my dad called another dad a clown outside school, I could crawl under a rock». (Kieran means he's very embarrassed.) What's true? | You can love your dad and still see he got it wrong | Your dad is always right | It's your fault he shouted | You should go and shout at the other dad too, to back him up
test: «Mrs Platt is pure evil, she moans about everything». What did the icy day show? | Grumpy people can be very kind when it matters | She was only being nosy | She wanted something new to complain about, like the leg | Grumpy people are never kind
test: What would probably happen if nobody had helped Mr Patel after the break-in? | He'd feel alone and the shop might struggle | He'd be happier on his own | The thief would feel bad and bring the till back | Nothing would be different
test: «the bus driver asked if I'm alright, cringe». Why does it matter? | Small friendly words help on a bad day | It doesn't matter at all | He's just being nosy about Jake's life | He wants Jake off his bus
test: «Eat them hot, mija». What is Mrs Lopez doing? | Sharing food, which builds friendship | Selling her tamales to the family | Asking Mia to cook | Telling Mia off for leaving them to go cold
test: In a cartoon, the shopkeeper chases a kid thief with a broom. What did Mr Patel do instead? | Let him make it right by sweeping | Chased him with a broom | Called his whole school | Banned the street from the shop
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dispute between the two elderly neighbours, Mr Shaw and Mr Kowalski? | A long silence of uncertain origin, concluded after a bereavement | A legal action concerning the ownership of a dog | A dispute concerning a sum of money owed by one to the other | A quarrel which persisted until the death of both
exam: Identify the principal conduct of the residents of the street following the burglary of the corner shop. | They assembled voluntarily to clean and repair the premises | They withdrew their custom from the shop | They held the shopkeeper responsible | They remained indifferent to the event
exam: Ascertain the arrangement made by the shopkeeper upon apprehending a child in the act of stealing a chocolate bar. | The child was to sweep the shop each Saturday for one month | The child was to be reported forthwith to the police | The child was to be excluded from the shop permanently thereafter | The child was to retain the item without consequence
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a neighbour who habitually complains. | She may nonetheless act with great kindness in an emergency | Such persons are incapable of kindness | Her complaints prove her to be an enemy | She ought to be excluded from the community
